{"name":"Common Chalcedony","hex":"#c8ad7f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"common","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Mutual; shared by more than one.","(of a quality) Existing among virtually all people; universal."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Mutual good, shared by more than one.","A tract of land in common ownership; common land."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/common"},{"term":"chalcedony","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A form of fine-grained quartz that is nearly transparent or has a milky translucence; it fractures conchoidally."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/chalcedony"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chalcedony","blocks":[{"text":"Chalcedony is an aggregate of cryptocrystalline or microcrystalline silica, or in simpler terms, a mass of individual silica crystals that are too small to be seen without magnification. It is composed primarily of quartz, with minor amounts of intergrown moganite. These are both silica minerals with a chemical composition of SiO2, but they differ in that quartz has a more symmetrical crystal structure (trigonal) than moganite (monoclinic). Mineralogically speaking, the quartz and moganite crystals in chalcedony must grow in parallel chains, called \"fibers.\" However, the term chalcedony may more broadly refer to any microcrystalline or cryptocrystalline silica, fibrous or not."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Quartz-46809.jpg/330px-Quartz-46809.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chalcedony"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Mog%C3%A1nite-798422.jpg/500px-Mog%C3%A1nite-798422.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Mog%C3%A1nite-798422.jpg/500px-Mog%C3%A1nite-798422.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A rare pseudomorph of a spiral Turritella-like snail shell that has been replaced by chalcedony","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMog%C3%A1nite-798422.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Sumin_chalcedony_mouse.jpg/500px-Sumin_chalcedony_mouse.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Sumin_chalcedony_mouse.jpg/1280px-Sumin_chalcedony_mouse.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Sumin_chalcedony_mouse.jpg/500px-Sumin_chalcedony_mouse.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Chalcedony mouse, by Avenir Sumin","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASumin_chalcedony_mouse.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Quartz_-_Agateplate%2C_redbrown-white.jpg/500px-Quartz_-_Agateplate%2C_redbrown-white.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Quartz_-_Agateplate%2C_redbrown-white.jpg/1280px-Quartz_-_Agateplate%2C_redbrown-white.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Quartz_-_Agateplate%2C_redbrown-white.jpg/500px-Quartz_-_Agateplate%2C_redbrown-white.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Agate","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AQuartz_-_Agateplate%2C_redbrown-white.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Carnelian_sard_%28mineral_specimen%29.jpg/500px-Carnelian_sard_%28mineral_specimen%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commo